+//! The **orphan-identity cleanup pass**: the walk whose completion opens the migration gate
+//! (proposal 0016 decision 4 rule 2, `0016:1251-1281`; the gate and its marker,
+//! [`wyrd_core::orphan_identity`]).
+//!
+//! GC's keyed protection — a mark whose event's `retire:bytes:` obligation is present protects
+//! its fragment, an absent one lets the mark's own grace govern (`crate::gc`,
+//! `retirement_draining`) — is sound only once **a fragment's mark names its most recent
+//! unreference event**. A mark that predates the event that actually unreferenced its fragment
+//! (a mark an older custodian wrote on a live fragment, one a metadata restore brought back)
+//! would let GC reclaim on that old stamp before the retirement drain re-stamps the position.
+//! This pass clears that population once, page by page, and writes the completion marker; until
+//! a valid marker is observed the routing of `unlink` / supersede through one obligation stays
+//! off ([`wyrd_core::metadata::unlink`]) and GC treats every mark as protecting
+//! ([`crate::gc`]).
+//!
+//! # Where it runs
+//!
+//! On the **reconstruction** input, after the retirement leg ([`crate::reconcile_step`]): the
+//! input the deployed loop supplies every interval (`crates/server/src/custodian.rs`,
+//! `run_reconstruction_until`). Only while no valid marker is observed: a pass that finds one
+//! makes two keyed reads (the marker, the restore-fence record) and answers
+//! [`Reconciled::Satisfied`].
+//!
+//! # Each mark, judged by what names its fragment
+//!
+//! A pass first reads who names every fragment position — the same reading GC protects by, in
+//! GC's order (`0016:782-800`): each listed upload session's owned `sidx:` entries, then its
+//! committed `part:` records (and, for a `Completed` session, which of them its pending
+//! `retire:records:` obligation publishes), then every committed inode (a segmented one through
+//! the shared resolver, and its `seg:` records), then every pending `retire:bytes:` obligation's
+//! own chunk list or segment range. Then each `orphan:` mark of its window, in key order:
+//!
+//! | The mark | Its fragment named by | The pass |
+//! |---|---|---|
+//! | `reclaiming` | anything | leaves it: no writer touches one (`metadata.rs`, [`wyrd_core::metadata::OrphanMark`]) |
+//! | its event's `retire:bytes:` obligation present | anything | leaves it: a drain mid-retirement, which GC's keyed lookup already protects |
+//! | any other | a committed map | **drops** it |
+//! | any other | a pending `retire:bytes:` obligation's own list | **drops** it: the obligation is the fragment's most recent unreference event, and its drain marks the position before it lets go |
+//! | any other | only an `sidx:` entry or a `part:` record of a live (`Open`, `Completing`) session — a part no Complete published — or of a session whose record will not decode, which nothing can move | **drops** it: nothing has unreferenced the fragment yet, so the mark evidences nothing, and a later publication and routed delete would otherwise be judged by it |
+//! | any other | only a **published** part's `part:` record (its number in a pending `retire:records:` `{parts}`) | **keeps** it byte-identical: it is the evidence of the delete that unreferenced the object, and the records drain deletes those records without marking (the 2026-09-12 trap) |
+//! | any other | only a record of an ended session that is not a published part — an `Aborting` session's entries and parts, a `Completed` session's residue and unnamed parts | leaves it: nothing publishes those chunks any more, and the session's own drain (its teardown `retire:bytes:`, its owned walk) re-stamps every position before it deletes the record — leaving also keeps the marks that drain writes ahead of its deletes |
+//! | any other | nothing | leaves it: GC's ordinary input |
+//!
+//! A mark that is none of the three shapes, or a key that is not one [`orphan_key`] spells, is
+//! left as GC leaves it, and named there. **No mark is ever re-stamped**: no event identity a
+//! re-stamp could give is sure to be the one that later unreferences the fragment.
+//!
+//! The pending-obligation row is this pass's own addition to `0016`'s list: a mark on a fragment
+//! named only by an obligation installed before the walk reached it (a multipart Complete's
+//! superseded generation, say) predates that obligation, and left in place it is exactly the
+//! stale mark GC's keyed lookup would reclaim on once the gate opens.
+//!
+//! # Every drop is conditional
+//!
+//! One commit per drop, carrying `require` on the mark's exact bytes and on the exact bytes of
+//! every record the judgement read, each **read again** and checked to still name the position:
+//! the inode, and its `seg:` record for a segmented map; the obligation, and its `seg:` record
+//! for a segmented generation; the `sidx:` or `part:` record and the session's `mpu:` record —
+//! that session still live (or still undecodable), since a Complete rewrites its record.
+//! The inline `unlink` deletes the inode and blind-puts its mark in one batch
+//! (`wyrd_core::metadata::unlink`), so it and the drop cannot both commit on the same reading. A
+//! drop that loses its precondition is judged again on the mark's fresh value, up to
+//! [`ATTEMPTS_PER_DROP`] times; one whose mark is gone, or now judged a keep, is settled. One
+//! whose record no longer names the position when read again — or that loses every attempt — is
+//! **unsettled**: the lap writes no marker, and the next lap judges it on a fresh reading.
+//!
+//! # Completion, and the marker
+//!
+//! A **lap** walks the ledger from its head to its end, a window per pass, under the
+//! restore-fence generation it began under ([`CleanupWalk`], at [`CLEANUP_WALK_KEY`]). It writes
+//! the marker only when it reached the end, **no** pass of it met a record it could not read,
+//! and every drop it judged settled. "Could not read" is GC's own test of an incomplete
+//! reference set (`crate::gc`, `ReferenceSet::unresolvable`, `StagedSet::unresolvable`): an
+//! inode that will not decode or resolve, a `seg:` record, a `part:` record, a session key that
+//! names no upload, an `sidx:` key that names no chunk — and, beyond GC's, a pending
+//! `retire:bytes:` obligation (whose positions this pass reads from it) or a `Completed`
+//! session's `retire:records:` obligation (which says what it published). Damage GC contains
+//! rather than counts as a hole — a placement that is not one server per fragment, an `sidx:`
+//! value that will not decode under a key naming its chunk, a session value that will not
+//! decode — names its chunk whole here too, and blocks nothing. The marker names that generation, and its commit requires
+//! the restore-fence record's bytes, the lap record's and the marker's as read, and deletes the
+//! lap record. A lap that met an unreadable record ends with no marker, naming the record
+//! ([`CleanupStatus::Blocked`], [`Reconciled::Blocked`]); the next pass begins a fresh lap. A
+//! pass that fails — a store fault, an unknown commit outcome — records nothing of its window,
+//! and the next pass judges it again. A restore since the lap began (a new generation) discards
+//! the lap.
+//!
+//! # Bounded per pass
+//!
+//! A pass reads the reference picture GC reads (one `inode:` scan, every session's bounded
+//! ranges, and the pending `retire:bytes:` ledger in pages), at most [`MARKS_PER_PASS`] marks in
+//! pages of [`LEDGER_PAGE`], at most [`READS_PER_PASS`] keyed reads for its judgements, and
+//! commits at most [`DROPS_PER_PASS`] drop attempts plus one write of its lap or marker. A window
+//! stops before a mark its remaining budget could not judge to the end, so the next pass
+//! resumes there.
+//!
+//! # Seeded DST
+//!
+//! deferred: #660 — seeded Tier-0 coverage of the walk racing `unlink` and Complete; the race the
+//! preconditions close is forced by a wrapper over a real backend in
+//! `tests/retirement_routing.rs` (F5).
+
+use std::collections::HashMap;
+
+use wyrd_core::metadata::{
+    self, decode_orphan_mark, orphan_key, parse_orphan_key, parse_seg_key, resolve_chunk_map,
+    seg_key, seg_range_prefix, ChunkMap, ChunkMapError, ChunkRef, InodeRecord, InodeState,
+    OrphanMark, SegmentGroup, SegmentRecord, ORPHAN_PREFIX,
+};
+use wyrd_core::multipart::{
+    decode_fence_generation, decode_owned_entry, decode_part_record, decode_retire_obligation,
+    decode_session_record, parse_mpu_key, parse_part_key, parse_sidx_key, part_range, retire_key,
+    retire_session_range, sidx_range, PartNumberSet, PartScope, RetireMode, RetiredMap,
+    SessionState, UploadId, MPUFENCE_KEY, MPU_PREFIX, RETIRE_BYTES_PREFIX, SIDX_PREFIX,
+};
+use wyrd_core::orphan_identity::{
+    decode_cleanup_marker, decode_cleanup_walk, encode_cleanup_marker, encode_cleanup_walk,
+    CleanupMarker, CleanupWalk, CLEANUP_WALK_KEY, ORPHAN_IDENTITY_KEY,
+};
+use wyrd_traits::{
+    ChunkId, CommitOutcome, DServerId, FragmentId, MetadataStore, Result, WriteBatch,
+};
+
+use crate::gc::{checked_page, object_name, staged_page, staged_placement};
+use crate::reconciliation::Reconciled;
+use crate::reconstruction::ReconstructionContext;
+
+/// The most `orphan:` marks one pass judges.
+pub const MARKS_PER_PASS: usize = 16_384;
+
+/// The most marks one page of the ledger walk holds. A mark is at most 73 bytes of key and 328 of
+/// value (`metadata::MAX_ORPHAN_EVENT_LEN`), so a page holds at most 411 KB.
+pub const LEDGER_PAGE: usize = 1_024;
+
+/// The most times one drop is attempted: a first commit, and one more after a lost precondition
+/// on the mark's fresh value.
+pub const ATTEMPTS_PER_DROP: usize = 2;
+
+/// The most keyed reads one mark's judgement makes: the obligation its event names, and per
+/// attempt a fresh read of the mark after a lost precondition and the (at most two) records the
+/// drop requires.
+const READS_PER_MARK: usize = 1 + ATTEMPTS_PER_DROP * 3;
+
+/// The most keyed reads one pass's judgements make.
+pub const READS_PER_PASS: usize = 4_096;
+
+/// The most drop commits one pass makes.
+pub const DROPS_PER_PASS: usize = 256;
+
+const _: () = assert!(READS_PER_PASS >= READS_PER_MARK && DROPS_PER_PASS >= ATTEMPTS_PER_DROP);
+
+/// What the cleanup's own records say of it: the answer an operator — or a test — reads instead of
+/// trusting the audit trail alone.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum CleanupStatus {
+    /// A valid marker under the current restore-fence generation: the walk completed, and the
+    /// identity-keyed retirement paths are on.
+    Complete,
+    /// No valid marker, and the current lap (if one has begun) met no record it could not read.
+    /// `unsettled` says it met a drop it could not settle, so it will end without a marker and
+    /// the next lap judges that mark again.
+    Walking {
+        /// The current lap met a drop it could not settle.
+        unsettled: bool,
+    },
+    /// The current lap met a record it could not read, named here by its escaped key: it writes
+    /// no marker, and the identity-keyed paths stay off until a lap meets none — needs a human
+    /// to repair the record.
+    Blocked {
+        /// The first record the lap could not read.
+        record: String,
+    },
+}
+
+/// Read the cleanup's status from its records: the marker, the restore-fence record, and the lap.
+/// Three keyed reads; nothing is written.
+pub async fn status(meta: &dyn MetadataStore) -> Result<CleanupStatus> {
+    let fence = meta.get(MPUFENCE_KEY).await?;
+    let fence = match fence.as_deref().map(decode_fence_generation).transpose() {
+        Ok(fence) => fence,
+        Err(_) => {
+            return Ok(CleanupStatus::Blocked {
+                record: object_name(MPUFENCE_KEY),
+            })
+        }
+    };
+    let marker = meta.get(ORPHAN_IDENTITY_KEY).await?;
+    if marker
+        .as_deref()
+        .and_then(|value| decode_cleanup_marker(value).ok())
+        .is_some_and(|marker| marker.is_current(fence.as_ref()))
+    {
+        return Ok(CleanupStatus::Complete);
+    }
+    let walk = meta.get(CLEANUP_WALK_KEY).await?;
+    Ok(
+        match walk
+            .as_deref()
+            .and_then(|value| decode_cleanup_walk(value).ok())
+            .filter(|walk| walk.is_current(fence.as_ref()))
+        {
+            Some(walk) => match walk.blocked() {
+                Some(record) => CleanupStatus::Blocked {
+                    record: record.to_owned(),
+                },
+                None => CleanupStatus::Walking {
+                    unsettled: walk.is_unsettled(),
+                },
+            },
+            None => CleanupStatus::Walking { unsettled: false },
+        },
+    )
+}
+
+/// One cleanup pass over the store `ctx` names (module doc). Answers [`Reconciled::Satisfied`]
+/// when a valid marker is already observed; [`Reconciled::Blocked`] while the lap has met a
+/// record it could not read; [`Reconciled::Changed`] when it dropped a mark or wrote the marker;
+/// else [`Reconciled::Partial`] — the walk goes on next pass.
+pub(crate) async fn reconcile(ctx: &ReconstructionContext<'_>) -> Result<Reconciled> {
+    let meta = ctx.meta;
+    let marker_read = meta.get(ORPHAN_IDENTITY_KEY).await?.map(|v| v.to_vec());
+    let fence_read = meta.get(MPUFENCE_KEY).await?.map(|v| v.to_vec());
+    let fence = match fence_read
+        .as_deref()
+        .map(decode_fence_generation)
+        .transpose()
+    {
+        Ok(fence) => fence,
+        Err(fault) => {
+            // No generation can be named, so no marker can be judged current or written.
+            emit_unreadable_record(MPUFENCE_KEY, &fault.to_string());
+            return Ok(Reconciled::Blocked);
+        }
+    };
+    if marker_read
+        .as_deref()
+        .and_then(|value| decode_cleanup_marker(value).ok())
+        .is_some_and(|marker| marker.is_current(fence.as_ref()))
+    {
+        return Ok(Reconciled::Satisfied);
+    }
+
+    let walk_read = meta.get(CLEANUP_WALK_KEY).await?.map(|v| v.to_vec());
+    let mut lap = match walk_read.as_deref().map(decode_cleanup_walk) {
+        Some(Ok(walk)) if walk.is_current(fence.as_ref()) && !walk.is_ended() => walk,
+        // Absent, damaged, ended, or begun under another generation: a fresh lap. A lap is a
+        // position and a verdict, never evidence, so starting again only costs a lap.
+        _ => CleanupWalk::begin(fence.as_ref()),
+    };
+
+    let index = Index::read(meta).await?;
+    for (record, fault) in &index.blockers {
+        emit_unreadable_record(record, fault);
+        lap = lap.with_blocker(&object_name(record));
+    }
+
+    let mut pass = Pass {
+        meta,
+        index: &index,
+        reads_left: READS_PER_PASS,
+        drops_left: DROPS_PER_PASS,
+        changed: false,
+    };
+    let (mut lap, reached_end) = pass.walk(lap).await?;
+
+    if reached_end && lap.blocked().is_none() && !lap.is_unsettled() {
+        let marker = CleanupMarker::for_fence(fence.as_ref());
+        let batch = pinned(WriteBatch::new(), CLEANUP_WALK_KEY, &walk_read);
+        let batch = pinned(batch, MPUFENCE_KEY, &fence_read);
+        let batch = pinned(batch, ORPHAN_IDENTITY_KEY, &marker_read)
+            .put(ORPHAN_IDENTITY_KEY, encode_cleanup_marker(&marker))
+            .delete(CLEANUP_WALK_KEY);
+        return Ok(match meta.commit(batch).await? {
+            CommitOutcome::Committed => {
+                emit_complete(&marker);
+                Reconciled::Changed
+            }
+            // The restore-fence record, the lap or the marker moved since this pass read them —
+            // a restore, or another custodian. Nothing of this pass's lap is recorded; the next
+            // pass reads where things stand.
+            CommitOutcome::Conflict => {
+                emit_marker_lost();
+                Reconciled::Partial
+            }
+        });
+    }
+    if reached_end {
+        emit_lap_ended(&lap);
+        lap = lap.ended();
+    }
+    let batch = pinned(WriteBatch::new(), CLEANUP_WALK_KEY, &walk_read)
+        .put(CLEANUP_WALK_KEY, encode_cleanup_walk(&lap));
+    // A lost precondition means another writer moved the lap since this pass read it: its record
+    // stands, and the next pass goes on from it.
+    meta.commit(batch).await?;
+    Ok(if lap.blocked().is_some() {
+        Reconciled::Blocked
+    } else if pass.changed {
+        Reconciled::Changed
+    } else {
+        Reconciled::Partial
+    })
+}
+
+/// `batch` requiring `key` to hold exactly `read` — the bytes this pass read there — or to be
+/// absent when it read none.
+fn pinned(batch: WriteBatch, key: &[u8], read: &Option<Vec<u8>>) -> WriteBatch {
+    match read {
+        Some(value) => batch.require(key.to_vec(), value.clone()),
+        None => batch.require_absent(key.to_vec()),
+    }
+}
+
+/// Which class of record names a fragment position — declared in the order the judgement ranks
+/// them, strongest first (module doc, the table).
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]
+enum Class {
+    Committed,
+    Obligation,
+    Staged,
+    Published,
+    Retiring,
+}
+
+/// One record that names fragment positions, with the keys a drop judged by it must require.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
+enum Namer {
+    /// A committed inode; for a segmented map, the `seg:` record that holds the chunk.
+    Committed {
+        inode: Vec<u8>,
+        seg: Option<Vec<u8>>,
+    },
+    /// A pending `retire:bytes:` obligation; for a segmented generation, the `seg:` record that
+    /// holds the chunk.
+    Obligation { key: Vec<u8>, seg: Option<Vec<u8>> },
+    /// An owned `sidx:` entry or a `part:` record of a live (`Open`, `Completing`) session, or of
+    /// one whose record will not decode — nothing has published or unreferenced its chunks yet —
+    /// with its session's `mpu:` key.
+    Staged { record: Vec<u8>, session: Vec<u8> },
+    /// The `part:` record of a part a Complete published.
+    Published,
+    /// A record of an ended session that a drain marks before it deletes: an `Aborting`
+    /// session's, or a `Completed` session's owned entry or unpublished part.
+    Retiring,
+}
+
+impl Namer {
+    fn class(&self) -> Class {
+        match self {
+            Self::Committed { .. } => Class::Committed,
+            Self::Obligation { .. } => Class::Obligation,
+            Self::Staged { .. } => Class::Staged,
+            Self::Published => Class::Published,
+            Self::Retiring => Class::Retiring,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// Which placement rule a record's chunks are read under: a committed map's, which admits the
+/// empty pre-M3 placement (`ChunkRef::checked_fragments`), or a staged record's, which does not
+/// (`crate::gc::staged_placement`). A placement either rule refuses names its chunk whole.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
+enum Rule {
+    Committed,
+    Staged,
+}
+
+/// Whether `chunk` names `slot` under `rule`: its placement places `slot`'s index on `slot`'s
+/// server, or the placement cannot be trusted and the chunk is named whole.
+fn names(chunk: &ChunkRef, rule: Rule, slot: (DServerId, FragmentId)) -> bool {
+    let (dserver, frag) = slot;
+    if chunk.id != frag.chunk {
+        return false;
+    }
+    let placed = |mut frags: Box<dyn Iterator<Item = (u16, DServerId)> + '_>| {
+        frags.any(|(index, placed)| index == frag.index && placed == dserver)
+    };
+    match rule {
+        Rule::Committed => match chunk.checked_fragments() {
+            Ok(frags) => placed(Box::new(frags)),
+            Err(_) => true,
+        },
+        Rule::Staged => match staged_placement(chunk) {
+            Ok(frags) => placed(Box::new(frags)),
+            Err(_) => true,
+        },
+    }
+}
+
+/// Where an upload session stands, for the records it names fragments by.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+enum Phase {
+    /// `Open` or `Completing`: a Complete may yet publish its parts.
+    Live,
+    /// A session record that will not decode. GC never decodes one — its records protect their
+    /// chunks through its key alone — so neither does this reading's completeness turn on it. No
+    /// Complete, Abort or drain can move such a session either, so nothing has published its
+    /// parts: its records are judged as a live session's, the drop pinned on its exact bytes.
+    Unknown,
+    /// `Aborting`: its teardown drain and owned walk mark every position before they delete.
+    Aborting,
+    /// `Completed`: its published parts' records go without marking; the rest are marked first.
+    Completed,
+}
+
+impl Phase {
+    fn of(state: &SessionState) -> Self {
+        match state {
+            SessionState::Open {} | SessionState::Completing { .. } => Self::Live,
+            SessionState::Aborting {} => Self::Aborting,
+            SessionState::Completed { .. } => Self::Completed,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// An owned entry's planned placement, in the shape the placement rules are stated over. An
+/// owned entry records no logical length, and neither rule reads one.
+fn planned(chunk: ChunkId, entry: &wyrd_core::multipart::OwnedEntry) -> ChunkRef {
+    ChunkRef {
+        id: chunk,
+        scheme: entry.staged().scheme(),
+        len: 0,
+        placement: entry.staged().placement().to_vec(),
+    }
+}
+
+/// Who names every fragment position this pass can see: the strongest namer per position, and per
+/// chunk for a record whose placement cannot be trusted, plus every record the reading could not
+/// read.
+#[derive(Default)]
+struct Index {
+    namers: Vec<Namer>,
+    positions: HashMap<(DServerId, FragmentId), usize>,
+    whole_chunks: HashMap<ChunkId, usize>,
+    blockers: Vec<(Vec<u8>, String)>,
+}
+
+impl Index {
+    /// Read the picture, in GC's order: staged, then committed, then pending obligations — each
+    /// move a transition makes (`sidx:` → `part:` → inode → obligation) leaves a chunk visible in
+    /// at least one class. A store fault fails the pass; a record that will not read is a blocker.
+    async fn read(meta: &dyn MetadataStore) -> Result<Self> {
+        let mut index = Self::default();
+        index.read_staged(meta).await?;
+        index.read_committed(meta).await?;
+        index.read_obligations(meta).await?;
+        Ok(index)
+    }
+
+    fn namer(&mut self, namer: Namer) -> usize {
+        self.namers.push(namer);
+        self.namers.len() - 1
+    }
+
+    fn block(&mut self, record: &[u8], fault: String) {
+        self.blockers.push((record.to_vec(), fault));
+    }
+
+    /// Record that namer `id` names `chunk`'s positions under `rule` — or the chunk whole, for a
+    /// placement the rule refuses. The stronger namer of a position is kept.
+    fn place(&mut self, id: usize, chunk: &ChunkRef, rule: Rule) {
+        let class = self.namers[id].class();
+        let frags: Option<Vec<(u16, DServerId)>> = match rule {
+            Rule::Committed => chunk.checked_fragments().ok().map(Iterator::collect),
+            Rule::Staged => staged_placement(chunk).ok().map(Iterator::collect),
+        };
+        let namers = &self.namers;
+        let keep_stronger = |held: &mut usize| {
+            if class < namers[*held].class() {
+                *held = id;
+            }
+        };
+        match frags {
+            Some(frags) => {
+                for (index, dserver) in frags {
+                    let slot = (
+                        dserver,
+                        FragmentId {
+                            chunk: chunk.id,
+                            index,
+                        },
+                    );
+                    self.positions
+                        .entry(slot)
+                        .and_modify(|held| keep_stronger(held))
+                        .or_insert(id);
+                }
+            }
+            None => {
+                self.whole_chunks
+                    .entry(chunk.id)
+                    .and_modify(|held| keep_stronger(held))
+                    .or_insert(id);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Record that namer `id` names every position of `chunk`.
+    fn place_whole(&mut self, id: usize, chunk: ChunkId) {
+        let class = self.namers[id].class();
+        let namers = &self.namers;
+        self.whole_chunks
+            .entry(chunk)
+            .and_modify(|held| {
+                if class < namers[*held].class() {
+                    *held = id;
+                }
+            })
+            .or_insert(id);
+    }
+
+    /// The strongest namer of `slot`: its own, or its chunk's whole.
+    fn namer_of(&self, slot: (DServerId, FragmentId)) -> Option<usize> {
+        let own = self.positions.get(&slot).copied();
+        let whole = self.whole_chunks.get(&slot.1.chunk).copied();
+        match (own, whole) {
+            (Some(a), Some(b)) => Some(if self.namers[b].class() < self.namers[a].class() {
+                b
+            } else {
+                a
+            }),
+            (one, other) => one.or(other),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Every listed session's owned entries, then its committed parts — published or not by its
+    /// pending `retire:records:` obligations — in GC's staged order.
+    async fn read_staged(&mut self, meta: &dyn MetadataStore) -> Result<()> {
+        let mut after: Option<Vec<u8>> = None;
+        loop {
+            let (sessions, next) = staged_page(meta, MPU_PREFIX, after.as_deref()).await?;
+            for (session_key, session) in &sessions {
+                let upload = match parse_mpu_key(session_key) {
+                    Ok(upload) => upload,
+                    Err(fault) => {
+                        self.block(session_key, fault.to_string());
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                };
+                let phase = decode_session_record(session)
+                    .map_or(Phase::Unknown, |record| Phase::of(record.state()));
+                self.read_owned(meta, &upload, session_key, phase).await?;
+                let published = if phase == Phase::Completed {
+                    self.published(meta, &upload).await?
+                } else {
+                    Vec::new()
+                };
+                self.read_parts(meta, &upload, session_key, phase, &published)
+                    .await?;
+            }
+            match (next, sessions.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(()),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The staged namer of a record of a session in `phase`: a live session's records are
+    /// staged — a Complete may yet publish them — while an ended session's are retiring, their
+    /// positions re-stamped by the session's own drain before anything stops naming them.
+    fn staged(&mut self, phase: Phase, record: &[u8], session_key: &[u8]) -> usize {
+        self.namer(match phase {
+            Phase::Live | Phase::Unknown => Namer::Staged {
+                record: record.to_vec(),
+                session: session_key.to_vec(),
+            },
+            Phase::Aborting | Phase::Completed => Namer::Retiring,
+        })
+    }
+
+    async fn read_owned(
+        &mut self,
+        meta: &dyn MetadataStore,
+        upload: &UploadId,
+        session_key: &[u8],
+        phase: Phase,
+    ) -> Result<()> {
+        let range = sidx_range(upload);
+        let mut after: Option<Vec<u8>> = None;
+        loop {
+            let (page, next) = staged_page(meta, &range, after.as_deref()).await?;
+            for (key, value) in &page {
+                match decode_owned_entry(key, value) {
+                    Ok((_part, chunk, entry)) => {
+                        let id = self.staged(phase, key, session_key);
+                        self.place(id, &planned(chunk, &entry), Rule::Staged);
+                    }
+                    // A value that will not decode under a key that still names its chunk names
+                    // that chunk whole, as GC's staged class holds it; a key naming no chunk is a
+                    // record this reading cannot place at all.
+                    Err(fault) => match parse_sidx_key(key) {
+                        Ok((_, _, chunk)) => {
+                            let id = self.staged(phase, key, session_key);
+                            self.place_whole(id, chunk);
+                        }
+                        Err(_) => self.block(key, fault.to_string()),
+                    },
+                }
+            }
+            match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(()),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The part sets `upload`'s pending `retire:records:` obligations publish.
+    async fn published(
+        &mut self,
+        meta: &dyn MetadataStore,
+        upload: &UploadId,
+    ) -> Result<Vec<PartNumberSet>> {
+        let range = retire_session_range(RetireMode::Records, upload);
+        let mut sets = Vec::new();
+        let mut after: Option<Vec<u8>> = None;
+        loop {
+            let (page, next) = staged_page(meta, &range, after.as_deref()).await?;
+            for (key, value) in &page {
+                match decode_retire_obligation(key, value) {
+                    Ok((_, _, payload)) => {
+                        if let Some(PartScope::Set(set)) = payload.parts() {
+                            sets.push(set.clone());
+                        }
+                    }
+                    Err(fault) => self.block(key, fault.to_string()),
+                }
+            }
+            match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(sets),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    async fn read_parts(
+        &mut self,
+        meta: &dyn MetadataStore,
+        upload: &UploadId,
+        session_key: &[u8],
+        phase: Phase,
+        published: &[PartNumberSet],
+    ) -> Result<()> {
+        let range = part_range(upload);
+        let mut after: Option<Vec<u8>> = None;
+        loop {
+            let (page, next) = staged_page(meta, &range, after.as_deref()).await?;
+            for (key, value) in &page {
+                let part = match parse_part_key(key)
+                    .and_then(|(_, number)| decode_part_record(value).map(|part| (number, part)))
+                {
+                    Ok(part) => part,
+                    Err(fault) => {
+                        self.block(key, fault.to_string());
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                };
+                let (number, record) = part;
+                let id = if phase == Phase::Completed
+                    && published.iter().any(|set| set.contains(number.get()))
+                {
+                    self.namer(Namer::Published)
+                } else {
+                    self.staged(phase, key, session_key)
+                };
+                for chunk in record.chunks() {
+                    self.place(id, chunk, Rule::Staged);
+                }
+            }
+            match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(()),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Every committed inode's chunks, resolved through the shared resolver as GC resolves them
+    /// (`crate::gc::referenced_fragments`); for a segmented map, each chunk under the `seg:`
+    /// record that holds it.
+    async fn read_committed(&mut self, meta: &dyn MetadataStore) -> Result<()> {
+        for (key, value) in meta.scan(b"inode:").await? {
+            let record: InodeRecord = match metadata::decode(&value) {
+                Ok(record) => record,
+                Err(fault) => {
+                    self.block(&key, fault.to_string());
+                    continue;
+                }
+            };
+            if record.state != InodeState::Committed {
+                continue;
+            }
+            let resolved = match resolve_chunk_map(meta, &key, &record).await {
+                Ok(Some(resolved)) => resolved,
+                Ok(None) => continue,
+                Err(err) => match err.downcast::<ChunkMapError>() {
+                    Ok(fault) => {
+                        // Name the segment record the fault is about, when it is about one, so
+                        // the operator is sent to the record to repair.
+                        let blocker = match (&record.chunk_map, &*fault) {
+                            (
+                                ChunkMap::Segmented(map),
+                                ChunkMapError::SegmentRecordUndecodable { index, .. }
+                                | ChunkMapError::SegmentBoundsMismatch { index, .. }
+                                | ChunkMapError::SegmentAbsent { index, .. },
+                            ) => seg_key(map.group(), *index).unwrap_or_else(|_| key.clone()),
+                            _ => key.clone(),
+                        };
+                        self.block(&blocker, fault.to_string());
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                    Err(err) => return Err(err),
+                },
+            };
+            match &resolved.record.chunk_map {
+                ChunkMap::Flat(_) => {
+                    let id = self.namer(Namer::Committed {
+                        inode: key.clone(),
+                        seg: None,
+                    });
+                    for chunk in resolved.chunks.iter() {
+                        self.place(id, chunk, Rule::Committed);
+                    }
+                }
+                ChunkMap::Segmented(map) => {
+                    let group = map.group().clone();
+                    let inode = key.clone();
+                    self.read_segments(meta, &group, |seg| Namer::Committed {
+                        inode: inode.clone(),
+                        seg: Some(seg),
+                    })
+                    .await?;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        Ok(())
+    }
+
+    /// Every `seg:` record of `group`, each one's chunks under the namer `namer` makes of its key.
+    async fn read_segments(
+        &mut self,
+        meta: &dyn MetadataStore,
+        group: &SegmentGroup,
+        namer: impl Fn(Vec<u8>) -> Namer,
+    ) -> Result<()> {
+        let range = seg_range_prefix(group);
+        let mut after: Option<Vec<u8>> = None;
+        loop {
+            let (page, next) = staged_page(meta, &range, after.as_deref()).await?;
+            for (key, value) in &page {
+                match metadata::decode::<SegmentRecord>(value) {
+                    Ok(segment) => {
+                        let id = self.namer(namer(key.clone()));
+                        for chunk in segment.chunks() {
+                            self.place(id, chunk, Rule::Committed);
+                        }
+                    }
+                    Err(fault) => self.block(key, fault.to_string()),
+                }
+            }
+            match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(()),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Every pending `retire:bytes:` obligation's own positions: a generation's chunk list or
+    /// segment range, a part attempt's chunk list. A `{parts}` or `{session}` component names its
+    /// positions through `part:` and `sidx:` records, read above.
+    async fn read_obligations(&mut self, meta: &dyn MetadataStore) -> Result<()> {
+        let mut after: Option<Vec<u8>> = None;
+        loop {
+            let (page, next) = checked_page(
+                meta,
+                RETIRE_BYTES_PREFIX,
+                "orphan-identity",
+                after.as_deref(),
+                64,
+            )
+            .await?;
+            for (key, value) in &page {
+                let payload = match decode_retire_obligation(key, value) {
+                    Ok((_, _, payload)) => payload,
+                    Err(fault) => {
+                        self.block(key, fault.to_string());
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                };
+                if !payload.chunks().is_empty() {
+                    let id = self.namer(Namer::Obligation {
+                        key: key.clone(),
+                        seg: None,
+                    });
+                    for chunk in payload.chunks() {
+                        self.place(id, chunk, Rule::Staged);
+                    }
+                }
+                match payload.generation().map(|generation| generation.map()) {
+                    Some(RetiredMap::Flat(chunks)) => {
+                        let id = self.namer(Namer::Obligation {
+                            key: key.clone(),
+                            seg: None,
+                        });
+                        for chunk in chunks {
+                            self.place(id, chunk, Rule::Committed);
+                        }
+                    }
+                    Some(RetiredMap::Segmented(group)) => {
+                        let obligation = key.clone();
+                        self.read_segments(meta, group, |seg| Namer::Obligation {
+                            key: obligation.clone(),
+                            seg: Some(seg),
+                        })
+                        .await?;
+                    }
+                    None => {}
+                }
+            }
+            match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(()),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// What the judgement of one mark decided.
+enum Verdict {
+    /// No write: a `reclaiming` mark, a drain mid-retirement, a fragment no record names or one
+    /// whose own drain re-stamps it.
+    Leave,
+    /// No write, and the mark is the evidence that matters: the 2026-09-12 trap.
+    Keep,
+    /// Drop it, judged by namer `usize`.
+    Drop(usize),
+}
+
+/// One pass's walk: its reading, its budgets, and what it did.
+struct Pass<'p> {
+    meta: &'p dyn MetadataStore,
+    index: &'p Index,
+    reads_left: usize,
+    drops_left: usize,
+    changed: bool,
+}
+
+impl Pass<'_> {
+    /// Walk one window of the ledger from where `lap` stands. Answers the lap moved on past every
+    /// mark judged, and whether the window reached the ledger's end.
+    async fn walk(&mut self, mut lap: CleanupWalk) -> Result<(CleanupWalk, bool)> {
+        let mut after = lap.after();
+        let mut marks_left = MARKS_PER_PASS;
+        while marks_left > 0 {
+            let limit = marks_left.min(LEDGER_PAGE);
+            let (page, next) = checked_page(
+                self.meta,
+                ORPHAN_PREFIX,
+                "orphan-identity",
+                after.as_deref(),
+                limit,
+            )
+            .await?;
+            for (key, value) in page {
+                if self.reads_left < READS_PER_MARK || self.drops_left < ATTEMPTS_PER_DROP {
+                    // The budget could not judge this mark to the end: stop before it, so the
+                    // next pass resumes here.
+                    return Ok((lap, false));
+                }
+                marks_left -= 1;
+                if self.visit(&key, value.to_vec()).await? {
+                    lap = lap.with_unsettled();
+                }
+                lap = lap.advanced(&key);
+                after = Some(key);
+            }
+            if next.is_none() {
+                return Ok((lap, true));
+            }
+        }
+        Ok((lap, false))
+    }
+
+    /// Judge the ledger entry `key` = `value`, and drop it if that is the verdict. Answers whether
+    /// it ended unsettled.
+    async fn visit(&mut self, key: &[u8], value: Vec<u8>) -> Result<bool> {
+        // Only a key a writer spells is a mark, and only a value of one of the three shapes:
+        // anything else is left exactly as GC leaves it, and named there (`crate::gc`).
+        let Some(slot) =
+            parse_orphan_key(key).filter(|&(dserver, frag)| orphan_key(dserver, frag) == key)
+        else {
+            return Ok(false);
+        };
+        let mut value = value;
+        for _ in 0..ATTEMPTS_PER_DROP {
+            let Ok(mark) = decode_orphan_mark(&value) else {
+                return Ok(false);
+            };
+            let id = match self.judge(&mark, slot).await? {
+                Verdict::Leave | Verdict::Keep => return Ok(false),
+                Verdict::Drop(id) => id,
+            };
+            let index = self.index;
+            let Some(pins) = self.verified(&index.namers[id], slot).await? else {
+                // The record the reading named no longer names the position: this reading is
+                // stale for it, and the next lap judges it on a fresh one.
+                emit_unsettled(slot, "namer-moved");
+                return Ok(true);
+            };
+            let mut batch = WriteBatch::new().require(key.to_vec(), value.clone());
+            for (record, bytes) in pins {
+                batch = batch.require(record, bytes);
+            }
+            self.drops_left -= 1;
+            match self.meta.commit(batch.delete(key.to_vec())).await? {
+                CommitOutcome::Committed => {
+                    self.changed = true;
+                    emit_dropped(slot, self.index.namers[id].class());
+                    return Ok(false);
+                }
+                CommitOutcome::Conflict => {
+                    self.reads_left -= 1;
+                    match self.meta.get(key).await? {
+                        None => return Ok(false),
+                        Some(now) => value = now.to_vec(),
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        emit_unsettled(slot, "precondition-lost");
+        Ok(true)
+    }
+
+    /// The verdict on `mark` at `slot` (module doc, the table).
+    async fn judge(&mut self, mark: &OrphanMark, slot: (DServerId, FragmentId)) -> Result<Verdict> {
+        if mark.is_reclaiming() {
+            return Ok(Verdict::Leave);
+        }
+        if let Some(token) = mark.retire_token() {
+            self.reads_left -= 1;
+            if self
+                .meta
+                .get(&retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &token))
+                .await?
+                .is_some()
+            {
+                return Ok(Verdict::Leave);
+            }
+        }
+        Ok(match self.index.namer_of(slot) {
+            None => Verdict::Leave,
+            Some(id) => match self.index.namers[id].class() {
+                Class::Committed | Class::Obligation | Class::Staged => Verdict::Drop(id),
+                Class::Published => Verdict::Keep,
+                Class::Retiring => Verdict::Leave,
+            },
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// Read `namer`'s records again and check they still name `slot`: the `(key, bytes)` pairs a
+    /// drop judged by them requires, or `None` when they no longer name it.
+    async fn verified(
+        &mut self,
+        namer: &Namer,
+        slot: (DServerId, FragmentId),
+    ) -> Result<Option<Vec<(Vec<u8>, Vec<u8>)>>> {
+        match namer {
+            Namer::Committed { inode, seg } => {
+                let Some(root) = self.read(inode).await? else {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                };
+                let Ok(record) = metadata::decode::<InodeRecord>(&root) else {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                };
+                if record.state != InodeState::Committed {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                }
+                match (&record.chunk_map, seg) {
+                    (ChunkMap::Flat(chunks), None) => Ok(chunks
+                        .iter()
+                        .any(|chunk| names(chunk, Rule::Committed, slot))
+                        .then(|| vec![(inode.clone(), root)])),
+                    (ChunkMap::Segmented(map), Some(seg)) => {
+                        if !in_group(seg, map.group()) {
+                            return Ok(None);
+                        }
+                        let Some(segment) = self.segment_naming(seg, slot).await? else {
+                            return Ok(None);
+                        };
+                        Ok(Some(vec![(inode.clone(), root), (seg.clone(), segment)]))
+                    }
+                    _ => Ok(None),
+                }
+            }
+            Namer::Obligation { key, seg } => {
+                let Some(value) = self.read(key).await? else {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                };
+                let Ok((_, _, payload)) = decode_retire_obligation(key, &value) else {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                };
+                match (payload.generation().map(|g| g.map()), seg) {
+                    (Some(RetiredMap::Segmented(group)), Some(seg)) => {
+                        if !in_group(seg, group) {
+                            return Ok(None);
+                        }
+                        let Some(segment) = self.segment_naming(seg, slot).await? else {
+                            return Ok(None);
+                        };
+                        Ok(Some(vec![(key.clone(), value), (seg.clone(), segment)]))
+                    }
+                    (generation, None) => {
+                        let flat = match generation {
+                            Some(RetiredMap::Flat(chunks)) => chunks.as_slice(),
+                            _ => &[],
+                        };
+                        let named = flat.iter().any(|chunk| names(chunk, Rule::Committed, slot))
+                            || payload
+                                .chunks()
+                                .iter()
+                                .any(|chunk| names(chunk, Rule::Staged, slot));
+                        Ok(named.then(|| vec![(key.clone(), value)]))
+                    }
+                    _ => Ok(None),
+                }
+            }
+            Namer::Staged { record, session } => {
+                let Some(value) = self.read(record).await? else {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                };
+                let named = if record.starts_with(SIDX_PREFIX) {
+                    match decode_owned_entry(record, &value) {
+                        Ok((_, chunk, entry)) => names(&planned(chunk, &entry), Rule::Staged, slot),
+                        // Undecodable under a key that names the chunk: it names the chunk whole.
+                        Err(_) => {
+                            parse_sidx_key(record).is_ok_and(|(_, _, chunk)| chunk == slot.1.chunk)
+                        }
+                    }
+                } else {
+                    decode_part_record(&value).is_ok_and(|part| {
+                        part.chunks()
+                            .iter()
+                            .any(|chunk| names(chunk, Rule::Staged, slot))
+                    })
+                };
+                if !named {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                }
+                let Some(mpu) = self.read(session).await? else {
+                    return Ok(None);
+                };
+                // Still live: a session that has left `Open`/`Completing` since the reading has
+                // published its parts or is retiring them, and the drop is no longer this
+                // judgement's. Its bytes are required, so a Complete racing the drop fails it.
+                let live = decode_session_record(&mpu)
+                    .map_or(true, |record| Phase::of(record.state()) == Phase::Live);
+                Ok(live.then(|| vec![(record.clone(), value), (session.clone(), mpu)]))
+            }
+            Namer::Published | Namer::Retiring => Ok(None),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The bytes of the `seg:` record at `seg` if it still decodes and names `slot`.
+    async fn segment_naming(
+        &mut self,
+        seg: &[u8],
+        slot: (DServerId, FragmentId),
+    ) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>> {
+        let Some(value) = self.read(seg).await? else {
+            return Ok(None);
+        };
+        let named = metadata::decode::<SegmentRecord>(&value).is_ok_and(|segment| {
+            segment
+                .chunks()
+                .iter()
+                .any(|chunk| names(chunk, Rule::Committed, slot))
+        });
+        Ok(named.then_some(value))
+    }
+
+    /// One keyed read, charged to the pass's budget.
+    async fn read(&mut self, key: &[u8]) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>> {
+        self.reads_left = self.reads_left.saturating_sub(1);
+        Ok(self.meta.get(key).await?.map(|value| value.to_vec()))
+    }
+}
+
+/// Whether `seg` is a segment key of `group`.
+fn in_group(seg: &[u8], group: &SegmentGroup) -> bool {
+    parse_seg_key(seg)
+        .is_ok_and(|(nonce, epoch, _)| &nonce == group.nonce() && epoch == group.epoch())
+}
+
+fn class_name(class: Class) -> &'static str {
+    match class {
+        Class::Committed => "committed",
+        Class::Obligation => "pending-obligation",
+        Class::Staged => "staged",
+        Class::Published => "published-part",
+        Class::Retiring => "retiring-part",
+    }
+}
+
+fn emit_dropped(slot: (DServerId, FragmentId), class: Class) {
+    let (dserver, frag) = slot;
+    tracing::info!(monotonic_counter.orphan_identity_marks_dropped = 1_u64);
+    tracing::info!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.orphan_identity.audit",
+        action = "orphan-identity-mark-dropped",
+        dserver,
+        chunk = frag.chunk,
+        index = frag.index,
+        named_by = class_name(class),
+        "the orphan-identity cleanup dropped a mark on a fragment a record still names",
+    );
+}
+
+fn emit_unsettled(slot: (DServerId, FragmentId), reason: &'static str) {
+    let (dserver, frag) = slot;
+    tracing::info!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.orphan_identity",
+        dserver,
+        chunk = frag.chunk,
+        index = frag.index,
+        reason,
+        "the orphan-identity cleanup could not settle a drop; this lap writes no marker and the \
+         next lap judges the mark again",
+    );
+}
+
+fn emit_unreadable_record(record: &[u8], fault: &str) {
+    tracing::warn!(monotonic_counter.orphan_identity_blocked = 1_u64);
+    tracing::warn!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.orphan_identity.audit",
+        action = "orphan-identity-blocked",
+        record = %object_name(record),
+        fault,
+        "the orphan-identity cleanup could not read a record it judges marks by; this lap writes \
+         no marker and the retirement routing stays off until a lap meets none — operator signal \
+         (NEEDS-HUMAN: repair the record)",
+    );
+}
+
+fn emit_lap_ended(lap: &CleanupWalk) {
+    tracing::warn!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.orphan_identity.audit",
+        action = "orphan-identity-lap-ended",
+        blocked = lap.blocked().unwrap_or_default(),
+        unsettled = lap.is_unsettled(),
+        "the orphan-identity cleanup reached the end of the ledger without writing its marker; \
+         the next pass begins a fresh lap",
+    );
+}
+
+fn emit_complete(marker: &CleanupMarker) {
+    tracing::warn!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.orphan_identity.audit",
+        action = "orphan-identity-complete",
+        fence_generation = marker.fence_generation().unwrap_or_default(),
+        "the orphan-identity cleanup completed and wrote its marker: deletes and overwrites past \
+         one batch now retire through obligations, and GC relies on its keyed lookup",
+    );
+}
+
+fn emit_marker_lost() {
+    tracing::info!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.orphan_identity",
+        "the orphan-identity cleanup's marker write lost its precondition (a restore or another \
+         writer moved the restore-fence record, the lap or the marker); the next pass reads again",
+    );
+}
